---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Rahul Kumar"
  description="I’m Rahul Kumar, a Senior Backend Engineer who writes about Java, distributed systems, cloud platforms, and practical software design."
>
  <section class="page-hero about-page-hero section-wrap">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Atlas', href: '/' }, { label: 'About' }]} />
    <div class="about-page-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="section-index">ABOUT ME</p>
        <h1>I build backend systems and share what I learn while doing it.</h1>
        <p>
          I’m Rahul Kumar, a Senior Backend Engineer at JPMorgan Chase with more than eight
          years of experience in backend, cloud, and platform engineering. Before this, I worked
          as a Member of Technical Staff at Oracle.
        </p>
        <div class="about-page-actions">
          <a class="button button-primary" href={SITE.linkedin}>Connect on LinkedIn</a>
          <a class="button button-secondary" href={SITE.portfolio}>View my portfolio</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="portrait-frame">
        <img src="/images/rahul-kumar.jpg" alt="Rahul Kumar" width="420" height="420" />
        <span>RAHUL KUMAR / SENIOR BACKEND ENGINEER</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="about-story section-wrap">
    <div class="about-story-label">
      <span>01</span>
      <p>A LITTLE ABOUT MY WORK</p>
    </div>
    <div class="about-story-copy">
      <h2>I enjoy the kind of engineering work where reliability and developer experience meet.</h2>
      <p>
        Over the years I have worked on Java and Go services, cloud infrastructure, build
        platforms, CI/CD systems, distributed caching, observability, and tools that help
        developers understand failures faster. I like problems where the answer has to work in
        production, not only on a whiteboard.
      </p>
      <p>
        I started The System Design Atlas because writing forces me to understand a subject more
        clearly. I want this to become the place where I connect DSA and Java fundamentals with
        Low Level Design, System Design, cloud platforms, AI Engineering, and the choices we make
        while building and running real systems.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="career-highlights section-wrap">
    <div class="section-heading section-heading-notes">
      <div>
        <p class="section-index">SOME WORK I AM PROUD OF</p>
        <h2>A few problems I have had the chance to solve.</h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="principle-grid career-grid">
      <article>
        <span>01</span>
        <h3>Faster build platforms</h3>
        <p>I helped build CI/CD and container build systems that reduced build and deployment time by as much as 50 percent. The platform used Terraform, AWS ECS Fargate, and Kaniko for scalable remote builds.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>02</span>
        <h3>More reliable services</h3>
        <p>I have worked on Java and Spring Boot foundations with worker pools, Redis Sentinel, rate limiting, circuit breakers, caching, and safety controls for fault tolerant services.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>03</span>
        <h3>Better operational visibility</h3>
        <p>I have built AI assisted diagnostics for CI/CD failures, observability pipelines with Prometheus and Grafana, and contributed to cloud migrations involving 50 TB of data across 15 brands.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="principles section-wrap">
    <p class="section-index">HOW I LIKE TO LEARN AND WRITE</p>
    <div class="principle-grid">
      <article>
        <span>01</span>
        <h3>Start with the why</h3>
        <p>I understand a topic better when I know the problem it solves before looking at the final implementation.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>02</span>
        <h3>Be honest about tradeoffs</h3>
        <p>Most engineering choices depend on context. I try to explain what we gain, what we give up, and when the decision changes.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>03</span>
        <h3>Keep it practical</h3>
        <p>Examples should help with interviews, but they should also connect to the way software behaves after it reaches production.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="toolbox section-wrap">
    <div>
      <p class="section-index">ENGINEERING TOOLBOX</p>
      <h2>Technologies I work with.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="toolbox-list">
      {['Java 8 to 21', 'Go', 'Spring Boot', 'Reactive Java', 'Distributed Systems', 'Microservices', 'AWS', 'Terraform', 'Docker', 'Kubernetes', 'Helm', 'ECS Fargate', 'Jenkins', 'CI/CD', 'Redis', 'MySQL', 'Prometheus', 'Grafana', 'GenAI', 'MCP'].map((item) => (
        <span>{item}</span>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="about-connect section-wrap">
    <div class="about-connect-card">
      <p class="section-index">LET’S CONNECT</p>
      <h2>If you are working on an interesting backend or platform problem, I would be happy to hear about it.</h2>
      <p>I enjoy conversations about distributed systems, Java, cloud platforms, developer productivity, practical AI tooling, and engineering careers.</p>
      <div class="about-page-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href={SITE.linkedin}>Connect on LinkedIn</a>
        <a class="button button-secondary" href={SITE.scalableStack}>Read The Scalable Stack</a>
        <a class="button button-secondary" href={SITE.topmate}>Book a conversation</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
